\DOC LOCAL_MONTMUL_SM2_TAC

\TYPE {LOCAL_MONTMUL_SM2_TAC : int -> string list -> tactic}

\SYNOPSIS
Advances an {eventually} machine-execution goal past one inlined
Montgomery-multiplication subroutine call, replacing the current symbolic state
with its successor and recording the multiplication's result as a new
assumption.

\DESCRIBE
The first argument is an {int} offset/index used to locate the call site within
the program; the second is a {string list} naming the three memory buffers
involved — the destination and the two operands — which identifies the stored
values that the new result assumption relates. Applied to a goal whose
conclusion is an {eventually} statement about a symbolic machine state, with a
large assumption list of the shape described below, the tactic always yields
exactly one goal. In the conclusion, the final state variable is replaced by a
fresh successor state variable, the {eventually} body itself being unchanged.
Every assumption mentioning the old state — the program-counter equation, the
{bytes_loaded} assumption, the register-value equations, the memory-content
equations and the accumulated {MAYCHANGE} frame — is discharged and re-added in
terms of the new state: the program counter is advanced, the {MAYCHANGE} frame
is extended by the destination buffer's component (when not already present),
and a memory-content equation for the destination buffer gives its new value.
One further arithmetic assumption of the form {t ==> u} is introduced, relating
the destination's new value to the Montgomery product of the two operand
values. Assumptions not mentioning the state — side conditions, non-overlapping
facts, and arithmetic facts added by earlier such steps — are retained,
possibly reordered.

\COMMENTS
The observed traces show only this single kind of transformation, always on
goals of the form just described.

\ENDDOC
